   "subject": "Filter by hardy-weinberg",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><div>Hi,</div><div><br></div><div>I have a question about the gl.report.hwe function.</div><div><br></div><div>I have been using it to identify loci that significantly deviate from hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. But in doing so, noticed that with the exact same input loci, I get a slightly different set of loci that are being reported as falling outside hwe. It&#39;s not that many (as few as 10 of ~2000) but I couldn&#39;t work out why this might be? Pardon the ignorance if I have missed something integral - but wasn&#39;t sure if this was correct or not....</div><div><br></div><div>Many thanks in advance!</div><div><br></div><div>Kasha</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "auIXWj75AwAJ",
   "from": "Arthur Georges",
   "author": "a3ea45e87285d820",
   "date": "2019-03-07T04:55:41Z",
   "subject": "Re: Filter by hardy-weinberg",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><div>Hi Kasha,</div><div><br></div><div>Can you submit the statement you are using to generate the departures from HWE.</div><div><br></div><div>I just tried</div><div><br></div><div>list &lt;- gl.report.hwe(<a href=\"http://testset.gl\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://testset.gl&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792458756672000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0ZIe_U8nRbPs7J6GqEdrjl\">testset.gl</a>,subset=c(&quot;EmmacMaclGeor&quot;, &quot;EmmacCoopCully&quot;))</div><div><br></div><div>but it has relatively few loci, and always gave the same set and levels of significance.</div><div><br></div><div>A<br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
